PR #107  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Unconditional entropy source requirement: systems with cryptographic extensions MUST provide hardware randomness, otherwise all cryptographic guarantees collapse due to predictable key generation.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:198-213  riscv_ext_vector_crypto_validate
      This is THE central gate for all vector-crypto extension validation (zvkned, zvksed, zvksh, zvkg, zvkn, zvks, zvkb, zvknha, zvknhb, zvkt, zvknc, zvkng, zvksc, zvksg). It only checks CONFIG_RISCV_ISA_V and ZVE32X availability — it NEVER verifies that Zkr (the entropy source) is present in the ISA bitmap. This directly enables the vulnerability condition: crypto_extensions_enabled AND entropy_source_missing → weak_randomness. A system with vector crypto accelerators but no hardware entropy source passes validation silently.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:540-577  riscv_isa_ext
      The extension registration table encodes no Zkr dependency for sub-bundles (zkn, zks) and individual scalar-crypto extensions (zknd, zkne, zknh, zksed, zksh). All vector-crypto entries (zvbb, zvbc, zvkb, zvkg, zvkn, zvknc, zvkng, zvkned, zvknha, zvknhb, zvks, zvksc, zvksed, zvksh, zvksg, zvkt) use riscv_ext_vector_crypto_validate which doesn't check Zkr. Only the 'zk' umbrella bundle (line 542) transitively includes Zkr. This is the static description that feeds the validate callbacks — the table itself encodes the missing dependency.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:333-337  riscv_zk_bundled_exts
      The 'zk' umbrella bundle correctly includes ZKR alongside ZKN and ZKT. A system advertising 'zk' gets the entropy source. This is the only correct bundle — the problem is that sub-bundles (zkn, zks) and individual extensions bypass it.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:339-348  riscv_zkn_bundled_exts / riscv_zks_bundled_exts
      The zkn sub-bundle (NIST scalar crypto) contains only ZKN (which expands to ZBKB+ZBKC+ZBKX+ZKND+ZKNE+ZKNH — no Zkr). The zks sub-bundle (ShangMi scalar crypto) contains ZBKB+ZBKC+ZKSED+ZKSH — no Zkr. Neither includes Zkr. A system advertising 'zkn' or 'zks' passes validation without an entropy source. Per the RISC-V crypto spec, scalar crypto extensions require Zkr. These arrays are the static-data root cause: the bundles that should include Zkr but don't.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:325-331  RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZKN macro
      The ZKN macro defines NIST scalar crypto as ZBKB+ZBKC+ZBKX+ZKND+ZKNE+ZKNH — scalar bitmanip and NIST cipher/hash accelerators with no Zkr. This macro is the building block used by both riscv_zk_bundled_exts and riscv_zkn_bundled_exts. It illustrates that ZKN is defined purely in terms of cipher/hash accelerators without any entropy requirement, contrary to the RISC-V spec which mandates Zkr alongside crypto extensions.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/archrandom.h:57-70  arch_get_random_seed_longs
      The sole runtime entropy entry point on RISC-V. Correctly gates on riscv_has_extension_likely(RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZKR) before calling csr_seed_long(). When Zkr is absent, returns 0 — zero hardware entropy. This is correct and safe behavior; the vulnerability is upstream (cpufeature.c allowing crypto extensions without Zkr), not in this function. The function honestly reports that no hardware entropy is available.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/archrandom.h:52-55  arch_get_random_longs
      Hardcoded to return 0 on RISC-V — there is no equivalent of x86 RDRAND in the RISC-V ISA. This is the honest/correct behavior. The consequence is that when Zkr is absent, both arch_get_random_seed_longs() and arch_get_random_longs() return 0, leaving random_init_early and extract_entropy with no hardware entropy contribution. This is a system-capability limitation, not a code bug.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/archrandom.h:17-50  csr_seed_long
      Low-level SEED CSR accessor using csr_swap(CSR_SEED, 0). Correctly implements the SEED CSR protocol: retries up to 100 times on BIST/WAIT states (with cpu_relax()), collects ES16 entropy values until a full long is assembled, returns false on DEAD (unrecoverable error). No runtime diagnostic distinguishing 'Zkr absent' from 'Zkr broken' is acceptable since the caller already gates on ZKR extension presence before calling. Properly handles the hardware polling contract.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:478-485  (CSR_SEED / SEED_OPST macros)
      Mere hardware constant definitions: CSR_SEED (0x015) and status bitmasks (BIST=0x00000000, WAIT=0x40000000, ES16=0x80000000, DEAD=0xC0000000, ENTROPY_MASK=0xFFFF). These match the RISC-V spec and have no logic to be wrong. Purely reference constants consumed by csr_seed_long().
  [RISK] drivers/char/random.c:857-892  random_init_early
      Core CRNG early init. On RISC-V without Zkr: both arch_get_random_seed_longs() and arch_get_random_longs() return 0 for every iteration, so arch_bits decrements to sizeof(entropy)*8 - ARRAY_SIZE(entropy)*sizeof(*entropy)*8 = 0 after the loop. The only entropy contributions come from _mix_pool_bytes(init_utsname(), ...) and _mix_pool_bytes(command_line, ...) — both largely deterministic. With trust_cpu=true (default), _credit_init_bits(0) is called. The function itself is architecturally correct (proper fallback chain); the risk is that cpufeature.c allows crypto acceleration on a system that provides zero hardware entropy to this function, leaving the CRNG seeded from weak sources.
  [RISK] drivers/char/random.c:663-685  extract_entropy
      Entropy extraction for CRNG reseeding with fallback chain: arch_get_random_seed_longs → arch_get_random_longs → random_get_entropy(). On RISC-V without Zkr, the first two return 0, leaving only random_get_entropy() (which may itself return 0 on embedded systems without cycle counters). The function is architecturally correct; the narrow edge case is when all three sources produce 0 on a system that also has validated crypto accelerators — the input pool is then seeded entirely from previously accumulated entropy with no fresh contribution. This is a downstream manifestation of the cpufeature.c bug, not a bug in random.c itself.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/crypto/aes-riscv64-glue.c:507-543  riscv64_aes_mod_init
      Registers AES ECB/CBC/CTS/CTR/XTS skciphers accelerated by Zvkned/Zvkb/Zvbb/Zvkg. Correctly checks for the crypto acceleration extensions it needs (ZVKNED, ZVKB, ZVBB, ZVKG). Does NOT check for Zkr — but this is not its responsibility. This is a downstream consumer of the vulnerability: when cpufeature.c validates these vector-crypto extensions without Zkr, this module provides high-performance hardware-accelerated symmetric crypto (including XTS for disk encryption) whose key material was generated from a weakly-seeded CRNG. The bug is upstream in cpufeature.c, but this is where the consequence materializes.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/pi/archrandom_early.c:19-30  get_kaslr_seed_zkr
      Early-boot KASLR seed collection. Parses the DT for 'zkr' before the kernel ISA bitmap is set up. Correctly returns 0 if Zkr is not in the DT string. Does NOT check whether crypto extensions are advertised — but this is correct: the function's sole purpose is to get entropy from ZKR, not to validate extension dependencies. If Zkr is absent (and crypto extensions happen to be present), KASLR is weaker, but this function correctly reports no-Zkr → no-seed. The gap is the same as everywhere else (cpufeature.c accepting crypto without Zkr), not a bug here.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_insn.c:102-110  seed_csr_rmw
      KVM intercepts guest SEED CSR access. Correctly checks guest ISA for ZKR and returns ILLEGAL_TRAP if absent. The risk is two-fold: (1) If the VMM doesn't implement entropy forwarding, the guest with ZKR-enabled sees KVM_INSN_EXIT_TO_USER_SPACE with no actual entropy delivered — crypto without entropy at the guest level even when the host has Zkr. (2) The VMM can configure guest ISA via ONE_REG to expose ZKND+ZKNE+ZKNH without ZKR, creating the vulnerability inside the guest. The KVM code itself does proper gating; the risk is architectural — no kernel enforcement that guest ISA crypto implies guest ISA Zkr.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:80-92  kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_extension_arr
      Exposes individual crypto extensions (ZKND, ZKNE, ZKNH, ZKR, ZKSED, ZKSH, ZKT) as independent ONE_REG entries. The VMM can set any combination via KVM_SET_ONE_REG — there is no kernel-side validation that crypto extensions imply Zkr. A malicious or buggy VMM can expose ZKND+ZKNE+ZKNH (full NIST scalar crypto) to a guest without ZKR, creating the exact vulnerability condition inside the guest: hardware-accelerated crypto with no entropy source. This is the nested-virtualization variant of the cpufeature.c bug — the KVM ABI permits the same invalid configuration.

