PR #107  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Unconditional entropy source requirement: systems with cryptographic extensions MUST provide hardware randomness, otherwise all cryptographic guarantees collapse due to predictable key generation.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:264-272  (macros CSR_SEED, SEED_OPTS, SEED_ENTROPY_MASK)
      Constants correctly encode the Zkr ISA spec: CSR_SEED=0x015, opcode field at bits 30:31 with BIST/WAIT/ES16/DEAD states, and 16-bit entropy mask. No executable logic — pure data definitions. Any spec change to seed CSR semantics would require coordinated macro updates, but the current encodings match the ratified specification.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:727-740  (macros CSR_MSECCFG, MSECCFG_SSEED, MSECCFG_USEED)
      Correctly encodes mseccfg CSR (0x747/0x757 for RV64) with SSEED bit at position 9 and USEED at position 8. These gate S/U-mode seed access respectively. Constants match the ratified privileged spec. Pure data — no logic to be wrong.
  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_hart.h:44-45  SBI_HART_EXT_ZKR (enum declaration)
      Sole internal identifier for Zkr entropy-source support. A single enum member in the sbi_hart_extensions enum — the single bit that gates all entropy-related decisions (mseccfg SSEED/USEED configuration, stack guard entropy init). Declaration only; correctness depends on consumers.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:368-399  sbi_hart_ext (extension name-to-ID table)
      The table maps extension name strings to SBI_HART_EXT_* IDs. Contains 'zkr' at line 376 but has no entries for Zk (scalar crypto umbrella), Zvkn (vector crypto NIST), Zvks (vector crypto ShangMi) — or any other crypto ISA extension. This means OpenSBI is architecturally blind to whether scalar/vector crypto extensions are present on the hardware. The firmware cannot enforce the security property 'crypto requires entropy source' because it never learns that crypto exists. If DT declares 'zk' or 'zvkn' without 'zkr', OpenSBI stays silent — it neither warns nor blocks, while S-mode independently parses the same DT and may proceed with cryptographic operations using weak/predictable randomness. The invariant that 'DT will always list zkr alongside crypto extensions' is not enforced anywhere in code.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:176-180  mstatus_init
      When SBI_HART_EXT_ZKR is set, enables mseccfg.SSEED (S-mode seed access) and disables USEED (U-mode seed access). This correctly follows the privilege separation model: trusted S-mode OS gets entropy; untrusted U-mode does not. The csr_set/csr_clear operations are atomic with respect to M-mode execution. However, correctness is entirely gated on the ZKR bit being accurate — this code has no independent validation that the SEED CSR actually returns entropy. The risk is that if the ZKR bit is falsely set (DT claims Zkr on hardware without it), SSEED is enabled on a non-functional CSR path. This would cause S-mode seed reads to trap, which is at least fail-closed (S-mode gets a trap, not fake entropy).
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:351-361  sbi_hart_has_extension
      Simple bit-test predicate: reads hart_features.extensions from scratch space and tests a single bit. The gate function for all Zkr-related decisions (mstatus_init, fw_base.S via scratch). No TOCTOU window because M-mode is single-threaded per hart. Correct as a predicate — the trustworthiness depends entirely on how the bit was populated upstream.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:634-679  hart_detect_features
      The CSR-probing block (lines 634-665) probes SCOUNTOVF, MTOPI, STIMECMP, MSTATEEN0, SSTATEEN0, MCYCLECFG, and TSELECT via trap-read — but does NOT probe CSR_SEED. Zkr is never confirmed via hardware trap-probe. Detection relies entirely on DT at line 679 via sbi_platform_extensions_init(). This is the central decision point: a DT claiming 'zkr' on hardware without a real entropy source will set SBI_HART_EXT_ZKR, causing mseccfg.SSEED to be set and fw_base.S to attempt entropy reads from a non-functional CSR. Conversely, hardware with a working entropy source whose DT omits 'zkr' will never have seed access configured. The invariant 'DT is always correct about Zkr presence' has no runtime enforcement. However, probing CSR_SEED via trap-read is architecturally problematic: (1) it consumes actual entropy, (2) SEED can legitimately return DEAD (entropy not ready) which is not an illegal-instruction trap, making the probe ambiguous, and (3) the RISC-V spec doesn't guarantee SEED exists at Priv v1.12 — Zkr is an unprivileged extension. The design choice to trust DT is defensible but undocumented, and the consequence of a wrong DT is the exact vulnerability condition in the spec.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:724-759  sbi_hart_init
      Top-level hart init orchestrator: clears mip, sets up trap handler, allocates scratch for hart_features, calls hart_detect_features() (which calls extensions_init → DT parsing), then delegates to sbi_hart_reinit() → mstatus_init() which applies MSECCFG SSEED/USEED configuration. No independent checks at this level. Correct sequencing — the issues (no hardware SEED probe, no crypto-without-entropy check) are in the callees, not here.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:711-722  sbi_hart_reinit
