  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_platform.h:479-484  sbi_platform_extensions_init (inline wrapper)
      If the platform_ops has no extensions_init callback, this wrapper silently returns 0 (success) without populating any DT-derived extensions. Consequence: Zkr is never set, SSEED is never configured, and the entropy source is inaccessible to S-mode — even if the hardware has a fully functional Zkr implementation. This is correct for platforms that genuinely lack Zkr hardware, but for a platform that has Zkr-capable hardware and simply forgot to wire up the callback (e.g., the template platform, or an incomplete third-party port), the entropy source is silently disabled with no diagnostic. The invariant 'every platform with Zkr hardware correctly implements extensions_init' is unchecked. The silent-0-return design means the failure mode is invisible.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:110-132  _start (stack guard initialization)
      Only consumer of CSR_SEED in the entire codebase. Reads entropy via ES16 opcode to initialize __stack_chk_guard. Correctly interprets seed opcode status: BIST (retry), WAIT (retry), ES16 (accumulate), DEAD (abort). Sets MTVEC to a recovery label so any trap during seed access falls through to the hardcoded canary. Two concerns: (1) The BIST/WAIT retry loop has no iteration bound — a stuck entropy source causes infinite spin at boot with no watchdog. In practice BIST/WAIT are transient, but a hardware defect could hang the boot. (2) The code does not check sbi_hart_has_extension(SBI_HART_EXT_ZKR) before attempting SEED access — it tries unconditionally and relies on the MTVEC trap-and-recover pattern. This is a 'try and fall back' strategy which is functionally correct but means the fallback is exercised on all hardware without Zkr (the majority), making the predictable canary the common case.
  [RISK] firmware/fw_base.S:776-782  __stack_chk_guard (initial value)
      Hardcoded fallback stack canary 0x95B5FF5A — used when SEED CSR is unavailable (trap), broken (DEAD), or the platform simply lacks Zkr. This is a compile-time constant visible in the OpenSBI source and identical across all builds. Under the vulnerability condition (crypto present, entropy source absent), every instance of OpenSBI on affected hardware uses the same predictable canary. An attacker who knows the OpenSBI version can forge stack canary values. However, the threat model is narrow: (a) it requires an M-mode stack buffer overflow in OpenSBI itself, (b) the attacker would need to already be executing in M-mode or have a separate M-mode vulnerability, and (c) the alternative — no canary at all — is strictly worse. The real issue is that the fallback is exercised on all hardware without Zkr, which is the common case today.
  [OK] firmware/payloads/test_head.S:109-115  __stack_chk_guard (test payload)
      Test payload defines __stack_chk_guard with the same static value 0x95B5FF5A — no entropy initialization at all. This is correct for test infrastructure: test payloads don't need production entropy, and spending SEED reads in test contexts wastes entropy and adds boot latency. Non-production code; does not contribute to the vulnerability condition.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:316-325  __sbi_hart_update_extension
      Trivial static inline helper wrapping __set_bit/__clear_bit on the extensions bitmap. The mechanism by which Zkr gets set (from CSR probing or DT parsing) or cleared. No concurrency concerns because M-mode per-hart execution is single-threaded. Low review priority; correct as a primitive.
  [OK] platform/generic/allwinner/sun20i-d1.c:200-204  sun20i_d1_extensions_init
      Platform override that chains to generic_extensions_init(), then registers a PMU device. Inherits the same Zkr detection gap as the generic path. Representative of the pattern used by andes_pmu, thead, and sophgo sg2042 overrides — all delegate to the generic path for ISA extension detection. Correct delegation; the issues are upstream.
  [RISK] platform/template/platform.c:117-122  platform_ops (template)
      Template platform_ops has no .extensions_init callback. The sbi_platform_extensions_init() wrapper returns 0 without populating any extensions. Zkr is never detected, SSEED is never set. Any template-based platform with Zkr-capable hardware silently lacks entropy source enablement. While this is documented as a template meant to be customized, there is no compile-time assertion or default handler that warns when extensions_init is missing. A platform author who copies the template and only fills in the obvious callbacks (early_init, final_init, timer_init) will ship a firmware that blocks seed CSR access even on hardware that has a working entropy source.

